      "solution": "a=b=1;exec(\"a,b=b,a+b+1;\"*int(input()));print(a)",
      "statement": "The Fibonacci sequence (0, 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13, 21, ...) is a famous sequence where each number is the sum of the two preceding ones. Computing the [[n]]ᵀᴴ (0-indexed) number of this sequence can be done with a recursive function <<fib()>>, where <<fib(>>0<<)>> = 0, <<fib(>>1<<)>> = <<fib(>>2<<)>> = 1, <<fib(>>3<<)>> = 2, etc. The pseudocode for <<fib()>> can be written as follows:\n\n```function fib(n) {\n    if n = 0 or n = 1 then return n\n    else return fib(n-1) + fib(n-2)\n}```\nA call to <<fib()>> for some value [[n]] calls <<fib()>> again for values [[n]]-1 and [[n]]-2, which then calls <<fib()>> again for values [[n]]-2, [[n]]-3 (x2), and [[n]]-4, and so on. This continues until the value passed to <<fib()>> is either {{0}} or {{1}}, where <<fib()>> is able to return the value immediately without having to make more calls to itself.\n\nGiven the value of [[n]], how many calls to <<fib()>> does it take to compute the [[n]]ᵀᴴ Fibonacci number? As an example, when [[n]] = 4, there are <<9>> calls to <<fib()>> in total (see below).\n\n```                fib(4)\n               /      \\\n         fib(3)        fib(2)\n        /     \\       /      \\\n    fib(2)  fib(1) fib(1)   fib(0)\n   /      \\\nfib(1)   fib(0)```",
